#include <LEDA/graph.h>
#include <LEDA/node_pq.h>
void DIJKSTRA(const graph& G, node s, const edge_array<double>& cost,
node_array<double>& dist)
{ node_pq<double> PQ(G);
node v;
dist[s] = 0;
forall_nodes(v,G)
{ if (v != s) dist[v] = MAXDOUBLE;
PQ.insert(v,dist[v]);
}
while (!PQ.empty()) {
node u = PQ.del_min();
edge e;
forall_out_edges(e,u) {
v = target(e);

double c = dist[u] + cost[e];
if (c < dist[v]) {
PQ.decrease_p(v,c);
dist[v] = c;
}
}
}
}
We start by including the graph and the node priority queue data types. The function
DIJKSTRA takes a graph G, a node s, an edge array cost, and a node array dist. Edge
arrays and node arrays are arrays indexed by edges and nodes, respectively. We declare
a priority queue PQ for the nodes of graph G. It stores pairs (v, dist[v]) and is initially
empty. The forall nodes-loop initializes dist and PQ. In the main loop we repeatedly select
a pair (u, dist[u]) with minimal distance value and then iterate over all out-going edges to
update distance values of neighboring vertices.
